cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this dataset, please cite it as below."
type: dataset
title: "AI-attributed physics-equation changes in open-source physics libraries: inventory, invariant specifications, Lean transcriptions, and native replays"
authors:
  - family-names: Jing
    given-names: Jiawei
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0000-8365-367X"
    affiliation: "Makuhari Development Corporation"
repository-code: "https://github.com/ibarapascal/physics-pr-lean-audit"
# Data and documentation: CC BY 4.0 (LICENSE-DATA). Code: MIT (LICENSE); exceptions in THIRD_PARTY.md.
license: CC-BY-4.0
keywords:
  - AI coding agents
  - scientific software
  - physics libraries
  - invariants
  - Lean 4
  - formal verification
  - differential testing
  - Modelica
abstract: >-
  Inventory, specifications, Lean 4 transcriptions and native replays for merged pull requests in open-source
  physics and engineering libraries that carry an AI-agent attribution. The inventory lists 1517 candidate pull
  requests (merged 2025-01-01 to 2026-10-16) found through the GitHub search API by agent account, PR-body
  signature and commit trailer, with two independent classifications of whether each change edits a physics
  equation. For a stratified sample of 12 code-level physics-equation changes (Cantera, ClimaAtmos.jl,
  Oceananigans.jl, CoolProp, pandapower, MOOSE, PyBaMM), the dataset provides three specification tracks of
  library-level invariants from two model families, a Lean 4 project (Mathlib v4.34.1) that transcribes the changed
  code and proves or refutes the invariants, native replay scripts and outputs that compare the installed
  libraries with the transcriptions, per-PR judgments, and 10^4-point native spot checks of each track's initial
  check plan on pandapower#3016. A controlled comparison re-poses five historical
  physics-equation changes in the Modelica Buildings Library and the Modelica Standard Library as change requests
  on pre-change library snapshots, with 10 generated patches per task and their regression and 10^4-point
  invariant spot-check results in OpenModelica.
